NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async queuing keeps the ordering, capacity, priority, expiration, and start or stop controls described in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queuing Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It adds controlled concurrency, Promise-aware processing, per-item retries, result callbacks, and control over active work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it when every accepted item should eventually run, but only a limited number of async operations should be active at once. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Rate Limiting"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when excess calls should be rejected by a time window instead of waiting."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-async-queuing-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How async queuing works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items move through two stages:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pending queue                 active work, concurrency: 2\n\n[ A, B, C, D ]    start A    [ A ]\n[ B, C, D ]       start B    [ A, B ]\n[ B, C, D ]    B finishes    [ A ]\n[ C, D ]          wait, C    [ A, C ]","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"concurrency"},{"type":"text","value":" limits automatically scheduled active items. Its default is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":". With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait: 0"},{"type":"text","value":", a free slot is filled after an item settles. With a positive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":", the queue waits that long after a settled item before checking for more work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue controls start order. With concurrency greater than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", completion order depends on the work itself."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-an-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose an API"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAsyncQueuedState"},{"type":"text","value":" for reactive pending items"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAsyncQueuer"},{"type":"text","value":" for concurrency, ordering, and lifecycle control"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"octane-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call the hook during component rendering. Removing the component runs its cleanup."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useAsyncQueuedState } from '@tanstack/octane-pacer'\n\nfunction UploadQueue() {\n  const [pending, queue] = useAsyncQueuedState(\n    uploadFile,\n    { concurrency: 2 },\n    (state) => ({\n      items: state.items,\n      activeItems: state.activeItems,\n    }),\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => queue.addItem(nextFile())}>\n      Upload ({pending.length} waiting, {queue.state.activeItems.length} active)\n    </button>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The focused snippets below use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAsyncQueuer"},{"type":"text","value":" inside a component or another hook."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":" when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started: false"},{"type":"text","value":" is set."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAsyncQueuedState"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[items, queue]"},{"type":"text","value":". Read the pending array during rendering and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queue.addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" to enqueue work."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ordering-pending-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordering pending items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The synchronous ordering rules still apply:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default adds at the back and reads from the front, producing FIFO order."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItemsFrom: 'back'"},{"type":"text","value":" for LIFO order."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItemsTo"},{"type":"text","value":" or pass a position to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" to control insertion."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPriority(item)"},{"type":"text","value":" to order higher numeric priorities first."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Priority ordering takes precedence over front or back removal."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  started: false,\n  concurrency: 2,\n  getPriority: (job) => job.priority,\n})\n\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'high', priority: 10 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'medium', priority: 5 })\nqueue.start()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The high and medium jobs start first. Their relative completion order is not guaranteed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"capacity-and-rejection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capacity and rejection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" limits pending items, not active items. Once an item starts, it leaves the pending queue and frees one pending slot. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" and calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" when the pending queue is full. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" is also rejected because the queue uses it internally to mean that no item is available."}]},{"type":"code","value":"if (!queue.addItem(job)) {\n  saveForLater(job)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capacity does not provide backpressure by itself because callers do not await an open slot. Handle rejection explicitly when dropping an item is unacceptable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"results-and-errors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Results and errors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatically scheduled work runs in the background. Observe results through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" or by selecting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":" through the adapter state. Observe failures through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" and selected error counters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For direct control, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes and processes one pending item. Its Promise resolves with the item that was processed, not the wrapped function's result. The result is passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" and stored as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". Background scheduling catches that rejection after updating callbacks and state so the queue can continue. A direct call to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" can reject to its caller. Providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" changes the default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess(result, item, queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" after a successful item."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError(error, item, queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" after its retries fail."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled(item, queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" after either outcome."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onItemsChange(queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" when the pending collection changes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject(item, queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExpire(item, queue)"},{"type":"text","value":" for items that never execute."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An item is removed from the pending queue before its function starts. A failed item is not automatically added back."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retrying-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retrying items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each started item receives its own retryer:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  concurrency: 2,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A retry remains part of the same active item and continues to occupy a concurrency slot. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAttempts"},{"type":"text","value":" includes the first attempt. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-retrying.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Retrying Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" before retrying jobs with side effects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"starting-stopping-and-flushing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Starting, stopping, and flushing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queues start automatically unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started: false"},{"type":"text","value":" is set."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" prevents new automatic starts and clears pending wait timers. It does not abort active items or remove pending items."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start()"},{"type":"text","value":" resumes automatic processing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes pending items. It does not affect active items."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush(count?)"},{"type":"text","value":" starts pending items immediately without normal wait spacing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flushAsBatch(fn)"},{"type":"text","value":" removes all pending items and passes them to one async batch function."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" uses direct executions and can start more work than the configured "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"concurrency"},{"type":"text","value":". Use it as an intentional drain operation, not as normal scheduling."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If any directly flushed item rejects with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError: true"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects after all requested executions settle. Remaining pending work resumes afterward when the queue is running."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"expiration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expiration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pending items can expire through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expirationDuration"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsExpired(item, addedAt)"},{"type":"text","value":". Expiration is checked when the queue ticks, not by a timer dedicated to each item. A stopped queue therefore evaluates stale items after it resumes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expired items are removed, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExpire"},{"type":"text","value":", and never reach the processing function. Active items do not expire."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"aborting-active-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aborting active work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort()"},{"type":"text","value":" aborts the retryers for all active executions. It does not clear pending items. Cancellation reaches the underlying API only when the processing function uses its signal:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queue = useAsyncQueuer(\n  async (job: Job) => {\n    return fetch(`/api/jobs/${job.id}`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      signal: queue.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { concurrency: 2 },\n)\n\nqueue.abort()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When multiple executions overlap, pass an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getAbortSignal()"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need a specific execution's signal."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"resetting-safely","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting safely"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" restores default state, including an empty pending queue and a running status. It does not clear the queue's wait timers or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use explicit lifecycle methods first:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queue.stop()\nqueue.abort()\nqueue.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"octane-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter stops automatic processing and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-and-reactive-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration and reactive state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility during component rendering and select only fields used by the view:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, { concurrency: 2 }, (state) => ({\n  size: state.size,\n  activeItems: state.activeItems,\n  status: state.status,\n}))\n\nconsole.log(queue.state.size, queue.state.activeItems, queue.state.status)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utility.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" with a selector and a JSX render callback for an independently subscribed child."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"concurrency"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" may be values or functions that receive the queue instance. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" merges new options, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncQueuerOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates reusable, type-checked option objects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":", they take precedence over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.isRunning"},{"type":"text","value":" likewise takes precedence over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started"},{"type":"text","value":". Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common state includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":": Pending work."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeItems"},{"type":"text","value":": Work currently tracked as active."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isRunning"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isIdle"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": Scheduler state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFull"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rejectionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Pending capacity state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"successCount"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"settleCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Execution outcomes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":": The most recent successful processing result."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekPendingItems()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekActiveItems()"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekAllItems()"},{"type":"text","value":" for copied item arrays. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-async-queuing-works","text":"How async queuing works","level":2},{"id":"choose-an-api","text":"Choose an API","level":2},{"id":"octane-example","text":"Octane example","level":2},{"id":"ordering-pending-items","text":"Ordering pending items","level":2},{"id":"capacity-and-rejection","text":"Capacity and rejection","level":2},{"id":"results-and-errors","text":"Results and errors","level":2},{"id":"retrying-items","text":"Retrying items","level":2},{"id":"starting-stopping-and-flushing","text":"Starting, stopping, and flushing","level":2},{"id":"expiration","text":"Expiration","level":2},{"id":"aborting-active-work","text":"Aborting active work","level":2},{"id":"resetting-safely","text":"Resetting safely","level":3},{"id":"octane-lifecycle","text":"Octane lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"configuration-and-reactive-state","text":"Configuration and reactive state","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nAsync queuing keeps the ordering, capacity, priority, expiration, and start or stop controls described in the [Queuing Guide](./queuing.md). It adds controlled concurrency, Promise-aware processing, per-item retries, result callbacks, and control over active work.\n\nUse it when every accepted item should eventually run, but only a limited number of async operations should be active at once. Use [Async Rate Limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md) when excess calls should be rejected by a time window instead of waiting.\n\n## How async queuing works\n\nItems move through two stages:\n\n```text\npending queue                 active work, concurrency: 2\n\n[ A, B, C, D ]    start A    [ A ]\n[ B, C, D ]       start B    [ A, B ]\n[ B, C, D ]    B finishes    [ A ]\n[ C, D ]          wait, C    [ A, C ]\n```\n\n`concurrency` limits automatically scheduled active items. Its default is `1`. With `wait: 0`, a free slot is filled after an item settles. With a positive `wait`, the queue waits that long after a settled item before checking for more work.\n\nThe queue controls start order. With concurrency greater than `1`, completion order depends on the work itself.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `useAsyncQueuedState` for reactive pending items\n- `useAsyncQueuer` for concurrency, ordering, and lifecycle control\n\n## Octane example\n\nCall the hook during component rendering. Removing the component runs its cleanup.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useAsyncQueuedState } from '@tanstack/octane-pacer'\n\nfunction UploadQueue() {\n  const [pending, queue] = useAsyncQueuedState(\n    uploadFile,\n    { concurrency: 2 },\n    (state) => ({\n      items: state.items,\n      activeItems: state.activeItems,\n    }),\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => queue.addItem(nextFile())}>\n      Upload ({pending.length} waiting, {queue.state.activeItems.length} active)\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe focused snippets below use `useAsyncQueuer` inside a component or another hook.\n\nPass `initialItems` when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless `started: false` is set.\n\n`useAsyncQueuedState` returns `[items, queue]`. Read the pending array during rendering and call `queue.addItem()` to enqueue work.\n\n## Ordering pending items\n\nThe synchronous ordering rules still apply:\n\n- The default adds at the back and reads from the front, producing FIFO order.\n- Set `getItemsFrom: 'back'` for LIFO order.\n- Set `addItemsTo` or pass a position to `addItem()` to control insertion.\n- Set `getPriority(item)` to order higher numeric priorities first.\n\nPriority ordering takes precedence over front or back removal.\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  started: false,\n  concurrency: 2,\n  getPriority: (job) => job.priority,\n})\n\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'high', priority: 10 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'medium', priority: 5 })\nqueue.start()\n```\n\nThe high and medium jobs start first. Their relative completion order is not guaranteed.\n\n## Capacity and rejection\n\n`maxSize` limits pending items, not active items. Once an item starts, it leaves the pending queue and frees one pending slot. `addItem()` returns `false` and calls `onReject` when the pending queue is full. `undefined` is also rejected because the queue uses it internally to mean that no item is available.\n\n```ts\nif (!queue.addItem(job)) {\n  saveForLater(job)\n}\n```\n\nCapacity does not provide backpressure by itself because callers do not await an open slot. Handle rejection explicitly when dropping an item is unacceptable.\n\n## Results and errors\n\nAutomatically scheduled work runs in the background. Observe results through `onSuccess` or by selecting `lastResult` through the adapter state. Observe failures through `onError` and selected error counters.\n\nFor direct control, `execute()` removes and processes one pending item. Its Promise resolves with the item that was processed, not the wrapped function's result. The result is passed to `onSuccess` and stored as `lastResult`.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`. Background scheduling catches that rejection after updating callbacks and state so the queue can continue. A direct call to `execute()` or `flush()` can reject to its caller. Providing `onError` changes the default to `false`.\n\nCallbacks include:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, item, queue)` after a successful item.\n- `onError(error, item, queue)` after its retries fail.\n- `onSettled(item, queue)` after either outcome.\n- `onItemsChange(queue)` when the pending collection changes.\n- `onReject(item, queue)` and `onExpire(item, queue)` for items that never execute.\n\nAn item is removed from the pending queue before its function starts. A failed item is not automatically added back.\n\n## Retrying items\n\nEach started item receives its own retryer:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  concurrency: 2,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nA retry remains part of the same active item and continues to occupy a concurrency slot. `maxAttempts` includes the first attempt. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before retrying jobs with side effects.\n\n## Starting, stopping, and flushing\n\nQueues start automatically unless `started: false` is set.\n\n- `stop()` prevents new automatic starts and clears pending wait timers. It does not abort active items or remove pending items.\n- `start()` resumes automatic processing.\n- `clear()` removes pending items. It does not affect active items.\n- `flush(count?)` starts pending items immediately without normal wait spacing.\n- `flushAsBatch(fn)` removes all pending items and passes them to one async batch function.\n\n`flush()` uses direct executions and can start more work than the configured `concurrency`. Use it as an intentional drain operation, not as normal scheduling.\n\nIf any directly flushed item rejects with `throwOnError: true`, `flush()` rejects after all requested executions settle. Remaining pending work resumes afterward when the queue is running.\n\n## Expiration\n\nPending items can expire through `expirationDuration` or `getIsExpired(item, addedAt)`. Expiration is checked when the queue ticks, not by a timer dedicated to each item. A stopped queue therefore evaluates stale items after it resumes.\n\nExpired items are removed, call `onExpire`, and never reach the processing function. Active items do not expire.\n\n## Aborting active work\n\n`abort()` aborts the retryers for all active executions. It does not clear pending items. Cancellation reaches the underlying API only when the processing function uses its signal:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(\n  async (job: Job) => {\n    return fetch(`/api/jobs/${job.id}`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      signal: queue.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { concurrency: 2 },\n)\n\nqueue.abort()\n```\n\nWhen multiple executions overlap, pass an `executionCount` to `getAbortSignal()` when you need a specific execution's signal.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, including an empty pending queue and a running status. It does not clear the queue's wait timers or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use explicit lifecycle methods first:\n\n```ts\nqueue.stop()\nqueue.abort()\nqueue.reset()\n```\n\n## Octane lifecycle\n\nThe adapter stops automatic processing and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing `onUnmount` replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed.\n\n## Configuration and reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility during component rendering and select only fields used by the view:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, { concurrency: 2 }, (state) => ({\n  size: state.size,\n  activeItems: state.activeItems,\n  status: state.status,\n}))\n\nconsole.log(queue.state.size, queue.state.activeItems, queue.state.status)\n```\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a selector and a JSX render callback for an independently subscribed child.\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\n`concurrency` and `wait` may be values or functions that receive the queue instance. `setOptions()` merges new options, and `asyncQueuerOptions()` creates reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\n`initialState` can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes `items`, they take precedence over `initialItems`; `initialState.isRunning` likewise takes precedence over `started`. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommon state includes:\n\n- `items` and `size`: Pending work.\n- `activeItems`: Work currently tracked as active.\n- `isRunning`, `isIdle`, and `status`: Scheduler state.\n- `isFull` and `rejectionCount`: Pending capacity state.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Execution outcomes.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful processing result.\n\nUse `peekPendingItems()`, `peekActiveItems()`, and `peekAllItems()` for copied item arrays. See the [Octane API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Octane Async Queuing Guide\nid: async-queuing\n---\n\nAsync queuing keeps the ordering, capacity, priority, expiration, and start or stop controls described in the [Queuing Guide](./queuing.md). It adds controlled concurrency, Promise-aware processing, per-item retries, result callbacks, and control over active work.\n\nUse it when every accepted item should eventually run, but only a limited number of async operations should be active at once. Use [Async Rate Limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md) when excess calls should be rejected by a time window instead of waiting.\n\n## How async queuing works\n\nItems move through two stages:\n\n```text\npending queue                 active work, concurrency: 2\n\n[ A, B, C, D ]    start A    [ A ]\n[ B, C, D ]       start B    [ A, B ]\n[ B, C, D ]    B finishes    [ A ]\n[ C, D ]          wait, C    [ A, C ]\n```\n\n`concurrency` limits automatically scheduled active items. Its default is `1`. With `wait: 0`, a free slot is filled after an item settles. With a positive `wait`, the queue waits that long after a settled item before checking for more work.\n\nThe queue controls start order. With concurrency greater than `1`, completion order depends on the work itself.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `useAsyncQueuedState` for reactive pending items\n- `useAsyncQueuer` for concurrency, ordering, and lifecycle control\n\n## Octane example\n\nCall the hook during component rendering. Removing the component runs its cleanup.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useAsyncQueuedState } from '@tanstack/octane-pacer'\n\nfunction UploadQueue() {\n  const [pending, queue] = useAsyncQueuedState(\n    uploadFile,\n    { concurrency: 2 },\n    (state) => ({\n      items: state.items,\n      activeItems: state.activeItems,\n    }),\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => queue.addItem(nextFile())}>\n      Upload ({pending.length} waiting, {queue.state.activeItems.length} active)\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe focused snippets below use `useAsyncQueuer` inside a component or another hook.\n\nPass `initialItems` when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless `started: false` is set.\n\n`useAsyncQueuedState` returns `[items, queue]`. Read the pending array during rendering and call `queue.addItem()` to enqueue work.\n\n## Ordering pending items\n\nThe synchronous ordering rules still apply:\n\n- The default adds at the back and reads from the front, producing FIFO order.\n- Set `getItemsFrom: 'back'` for LIFO order.\n- Set `addItemsTo` or pass a position to `addItem()` to control insertion.\n- Set `getPriority(item)` to order higher numeric priorities first.\n\nPriority ordering takes precedence over front or back removal.\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  started: false,\n  concurrency: 2,\n  getPriority: (job) => job.priority,\n})\n\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'high', priority: 10 })\nqueue.addItem({ id: 'medium', priority: 5 })\nqueue.start()\n```\n\nThe high and medium jobs start first. Their relative completion order is not guaranteed.\n\n## Capacity and rejection\n\n`maxSize` limits pending items, not active items. Once an item starts, it leaves the pending queue and frees one pending slot. `addItem()` returns `false` and calls `onReject` when the pending queue is full. `undefined` is also rejected because the queue uses it internally to mean that no item is available.\n\n```ts\nif (!queue.addItem(job)) {\n  saveForLater(job)\n}\n```\n\nCapacity does not provide backpressure by itself because callers do not await an open slot. Handle rejection explicitly when dropping an item is unacceptable.\n\n## Results and errors\n\nAutomatically scheduled work runs in the background. Observe results through `onSuccess` or by selecting `lastResult` through the adapter state. Observe failures through `onError` and selected error counters.\n\nFor direct control, `execute()` removes and processes one pending item. Its Promise resolves with the item that was processed, not the wrapped function's result. The result is passed to `onSuccess` and stored as `lastResult`.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`. Background scheduling catches that rejection after updating callbacks and state so the queue can continue. A direct call to `execute()` or `flush()` can reject to its caller. Providing `onError` changes the default to `false`.\n\nCallbacks include:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, item, queue)` after a successful item.\n- `onError(error, item, queue)` after its retries fail.\n- `onSettled(item, queue)` after either outcome.\n- `onItemsChange(queue)` when the pending collection changes.\n- `onReject(item, queue)` and `onExpire(item, queue)` for items that never execute.\n\nAn item is removed from the pending queue before its function starts. A failed item is not automatically added back.\n\n## Retrying items\n\nEach started item receives its own retryer:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, {\n  concurrency: 2,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nA retry remains part of the same active item and continues to occupy a concurrency slot. `maxAttempts` includes the first attempt. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before retrying jobs with side effects.\n\n## Starting, stopping, and flushing\n\nQueues start automatically unless `started: false` is set.\n\n- `stop()` prevents new automatic starts and clears pending wait timers. It does not abort active items or remove pending items.\n- `start()` resumes automatic processing.\n- `clear()` removes pending items. It does not affect active items.\n- `flush(count?)` starts pending items immediately without normal wait spacing.\n- `flushAsBatch(fn)` removes all pending items and passes them to one async batch function.\n\n`flush()` uses direct executions and can start more work than the configured `concurrency`. Use it as an intentional drain operation, not as normal scheduling.\n\nIf any directly flushed item rejects with `throwOnError: true`, `flush()` rejects after all requested executions settle. Remaining pending work resumes afterward when the queue is running.\n\n## Expiration\n\nPending items can expire through `expirationDuration` or `getIsExpired(item, addedAt)`. Expiration is checked when the queue ticks, not by a timer dedicated to each item. A stopped queue therefore evaluates stale items after it resumes.\n\nExpired items are removed, call `onExpire`, and never reach the processing function. Active items do not expire.\n\n## Aborting active work\n\n`abort()` aborts the retryers for all active executions. It does not clear pending items. Cancellation reaches the underlying API only when the processing function uses its signal:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(\n  async (job: Job) => {\n    return fetch(`/api/jobs/${job.id}`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      signal: queue.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { concurrency: 2 },\n)\n\nqueue.abort()\n```\n\nWhen multiple executions overlap, pass an `executionCount` to `getAbortSignal()` when you need a specific execution's signal.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, including an empty pending queue and a running status. It does not clear the queue's wait timers or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use explicit lifecycle methods first:\n\n```ts\nqueue.stop()\nqueue.abort()\nqueue.reset()\n```\n\n## Octane lifecycle\n\nThe adapter stops automatic processing and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing `onUnmount` replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed.\n\n## Configuration and reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility during component rendering and select only fields used by the view:\n\n```ts\nconst queue = useAsyncQueuer(processJob, { concurrency: 2 }, (state) => ({\n  size: state.size,\n  activeItems: state.activeItems,\n  status: state.status,\n}))\n\nconsole.log(queue.state.size, queue.state.activeItems, queue.state.status)\n```\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a selector and a JSX render callback for an independently subscribed child.\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\n`concurrency` and `wait` may be values or functions that receive the queue instance. `setOptions()` merges new options, and `asyncQueuerOptions()` creates reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\n`initialState` can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes `items`, they take precedence over `initialItems`; `initialState.isRunning` likewise takes precedence over `started`. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommon state includes:\n\n- `items` and `size`: Pending work.\n- `activeItems`: Work currently tracked as active.\n- `isRunning`, `isIdle`, and `status`: Scheduler state.\n- `isFull` and `rejectionCount`: Pending capacity state.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Execution outcomes.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful processing result.\n\nUse `peekPendingItems()`, `peekActiveItems()`, and `peekAllItems()` for copied item arrays. See the [Octane API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n"}
